        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix: isolate Synology webhook preauth throttling [AI]",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nFixes an issue where Synology Chat webhook traffic behind a trusted reverse proxy could throttle valid messages when invalid-token attempts came through the same proxy address.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nThe Synology Chat webhook handler now keys its pre-auth invalid-token limiter by the gateway-resolved client IP when `gateway.trustedProxies` is configured, while keeping the existing socket address fallback for direct traffic. Route startup passes the gateway proxy settings into the handler so the registered HTTP route uses the same client-IP contract as other webhook ingress paths.\n\n## User Impact\n\nOperators running Synology Chat webhooks behind trusted reverse proxies can keep valid webhook delivery isolated from unrelated invalid-token traffic sharing the proxy connection path. Direct, non-proxied deployments keep the existing socket-address behavior.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Added handler and route-integration regression coverage for trusted-forwarded client IP throttling.\n- Ran `pnpm exec oxfmt --write --threads=1 extensions/synology-chat/src/webhook-handler.ts extensions/synology-chat/src/gateway-runtime.ts extensions/synology-chat/src/channel.ts extensions/synology-chat/src/webhook-handler.test.ts extensions/synology-chat/src/channel.integration.test.ts`.\n- Ran `git diff --check`.\n\nAI-assisted.\n",
